<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/222-1879-us-dept-of-interior-cushman.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>After heavy lobbying of the territorial legislature by Mandan residents, Morton County (and its population count) would be restored to its original boundaries. The Missouri River railroad bridge which began operation in 1882 spawned rapid development of both Mandan, and the influx of immigrants to settle the rural areas in Morton County and points west. The citizens in what is today South Dakota wanted statehood in the early 1880s, but did not want to be burdened with the large unpopulated portions of the territory in the north. Congress insisted the south wait for enough population in the northern portion to also make it eligible for statehood. Both North and South Dakota were admitted to the Union in 1889.</p>

<h3>1880</h3>

<p>According to US Census reports, the population of Morton County was 198 males and 2 females. Recall in 1879, the most densely populated areas of Morton County including Mandan, Cushman, Lincoln and Fort Abraham Lincoln had been reassigned to Burleigh County. These areas would be restored to Morton County in 1881, but not until the "temporary" higher population recorded by the 1880 census likely helped Bismarck to win the 1883 competion to be the new territorial capital.</p>

<h3>1881</h3>

<p>On February 18, the Territorial Legislature restored Morton County to its original 1873 boundaries returning the previously annexed portion on the river's west bank back from Burleigh County. Areas including in the reassignment included Mandan, Cushman (i.e., the railroad bridge riverside work camp) and Fort Abraham Lincoln. Mandan was officially incorporated as a village on February 24.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/356-bingenheimer-drug-orginal-location-haynes-photo.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/289-maus-md-prescriptn-bingenheimer-drug.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Bingenheimer Drug opens (later becomes Taylor Drug) at 108 East Main Street and serves Fort Lincoln as well as the citizens of the Mandan area. The drug store would move two doors east to the Peoples' Hotel building by 1884 at Stark Ave and Main Street (today's Collin's Avenue &amp; Main Street).</p>

<p>Fredrick and Robert Hager moved to Bismarck during the Northern Pacific rail bridge construction. In March 1881, river ice jams and springtime floods brought ice cakes to Main Street. Fredrick watched from across the river on the high bluffs using binoculars to witness the devastation to east Mandan from flood waters. He understood the implications and seized the opportunity to rush to St. Paul and buy the available properties in west Mandan from the railroad. And returned with railcars of building supplies to take advantage of the opportunity to help rebuild the city further from the river.</p>

<p>Mandan Volunteer Fire Department formed. Herman Yunck was one of its first members. Carpenter-Cary Real Estate opens. On April 1, the first white child boy, Thomas Conroy, Jr. was born within the new city of Mandan. Thomas Jr. would later become a conductor with the railroad and be a long time resident of Mandan. (Note: Henry Helmsworth was born on June 14, 1878 in Mandan but before the settlement was officially incorporated as a village. Also a newspaper report claim that both Thomas Jr. and his older brother by 6 years William were born at Ft. Lincoln.)</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/400-blacksmith-shop-collins-ave.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Thomas G. Conroy, Jr.’s father, Thomas, Sr., was a blacksmith at Ft. Lincoln at the time of Custer’s ill-fated trip to Montana in 1876. He later moved to Mandan to establish a blacksmith shop in a log building just north of the former First National Bank building on Collins Avenue (formerly Stark Avenue.)</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/147-mandan-pioneer-newspaper-stamp.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The Mandan Pioneer weekly newspaper began operation on October 1. Initially it was owned by F.H. Ertel, previously from Jamestown, and Herbert H. Gove originally from Rochester, Minnesota. Gove would quickly sell his interest in the venture. By 1909, it went into more homes in Morton County than all other publications combined.</p>

<h3>1882</h3>

<p>In January 1882, The Mandan Pioneer replaced The Mandan Criterion, as the official newspaper of Morton County. The Criterion was published by Harry Robinson who had immigrated from Wisconsin, and continued to operate until at least 1887.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/371-the-daily-pioneer-1890.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In March 1882, The Mandan Pioneer became a daily newspaper. Mandan was the smallest city in the world to boast a daily newspaper.</p>

<p>Northern Pacific Railway completes its railroad bridge across the Missouri River. Main Street graded and board walks laid. Inter-Ocean Hotel, a three-story brick building, opened.</p>

<p>A new Queen Anne style passenger depot was completed in the Fall of 1882 at the cost of $12,000 ($400,000 in 2024 dollars).</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/285-1882-queenanne-depot-chropped.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The 100-feet long by 32-feet wide building had a brick first floor and was surrounded by an 8-foot platform sheltered by a wood canopy supported by ornate iron pillers. The wood second floor was accented by hipped wall-dormers for the windows. The Western Union office located there would prove office for up to nine telegraph operators on duty 24-hours a day. A cupola with a bell-shaped roof topped the building. This building retained the title of the finest railroad depot west of St. Paul for over a decade.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/188-1884-summary-mdnpionr.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>A special election was held in Mandan by the Morton County Commission to approve a $5000 expenditure ($170,000 2024$) to build an iron bridge south of the railyard across the Heart River. Not one dissenting vote. The 150-foot long bridge would open for traffic in 1882. Mandan experienced it's first major fire on November 29 along Main Street businesses. Mandan had no fire engine , but Mandan's superintendent telegraphed Bismarck's mayor for assistance. Within fifteen minutes, the Bismarck fire brigade was mobilized and hundreds of volunteers rushed to aid it's sister city. In thirty-five minutes, they had crossed the Missouri River by railcar and began dousing the fire with water from the rail car's engines. When the last flames were squelched, Mandan had lost three hotels including the Pacific Hotel and the Mandan House, Mack and Drury's restaurant, a hardware store, and a grocery house, totaling $17,000 ($565,000 2024$ dollars). Mandan's city commission would vote to purchase additional equipment for its fire department.</p>

<h3>1883</h3>

<p>Mandan's population surges to 2,500 with the completion of the river bridge and maintenance facilities for the railroad. Mandan claimed to have constructed more houses than any other in Dakota Territory that year.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/386-heart-river-bridge-cropped.jpg" alt="Heart River bridge">
    <figcaption>Heart River Bridge</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>With the opening of a bridge across the Heart River, the US Army changed its supply contract to require delivery to Mandan instead of Bismarck. The loss of freight traffic from the east side of the river forced the river ferries to lower their price, prompting more immigrants to arrive by water.</p>

<p>First telephone line was installed to the city. A group of Massachusetts investors under the name of the Mandan Land and Development Company, platted southwest part of the city south of the railroad yard. This "Boston Syndicate" would invest heavily in the southwest area of Mandan; even today the district is frequently referred to as "the Syndicate." The group began construction of a flour mill which would be incorporated as the ND Roller Mill. It would begin operation the following year.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/334-1883-hagers-blockdrawing.jpg" alt="Line drawing of Mandan in 1883">
    <figcaption>1883 Excerpt from J.J. Stoner Line Sketch of Mandan</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/334-1883-hagers-block-gilbert-stereoview.jpg" alt="Stereoview photograph of Mandan in 1883">
    <figcaption>1883 Excerpt from Gilbert Photographic Stereoview</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Henry Endicott bought the land at the corner of Ainsworth Avenue (today's 4th Ave NW) and Main Street to Henry Endicott. There he constructed the two-story brick "Endicott Building." Endicott was also a member of the "Boston Syndicate," a group of East Coast investors who developed the southwest section of Mandan.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/364-1956-mandan-creamery-produce.jpg" alt="Cloverdale Meats building on Main Street in 1956">
    <figcaption>1956 Cloverdale Meats Main St Building</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Among its first tenants was the Northern Pacific (NP) Bank. New York State Governor (and future US President) Grover Cleveland was its first President. The Endicott building would eventually be repurposed Mandan Produce and Creamery (later Cloverdale Foods/Cloverdale Meats) as their downtown processing facility, strategically located across the street from the railroad's warehouse. (The building was expanded several times before the business relocated to northwest Mandan. The building was demolished in 1988 and the site remains today as a parking lot.)</p>

<h3>1884</h3>

<p>The "Syndicate" built a brick yard in southwest Mandan. A rail spur which ran north and south from the railroad's east-west mainline was installed down to the Heart River to run between the flour mill and brick yard. A trolley service from the area to downtown Mandan across the railyard would eventually be installed.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/150-laird-kent.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The murder of Mrs. Julia (Laird) Kent in her home just south of the city monopolized the news. Although shot by the hired man, her husband Myron R. Kent (whose real name was William W. Pancoast) was the instigator of the crime. Out of town at the time of the incident, suspecision rose when he did not return for her funeral or his 10-year old son Earl. He was later apprehended in Colorado and brought back for trial. Found guilty, he was initially sentenced to be hanged at the <a href="/places/gone-forever/collins-street-county-courthouse/">Morton County Courthouse</a>. A later trial resulted revised the sentences to life imprisonment for both him and the hired man.</p>

<h3>1886</h3>

<p>Mandan, now five years old, is holding on to a population of nearly 2500. Main Street businesses included ten saloons and boarding houses, six restaurants, four grocery/dry goods stores, three doctors, two pharmacies, a Chinese laundry, a tailor and dressmaker's shop, several livery stables, two banks, four churches, two schoolhouses, a lumberyard, a brickyard, an opera house, a flour mill, a train depot and freight house, a hotel, postoffice, courthouse and jail, a racetrack, two four-page weekly newspapers and an undertaker.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/350-1913-morton-co-courthouse-and-jail-ps.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In January 1886, County Treasurer Bingenheimer and County Auditor Van Vleck and Clerk of Court Doyle moved their records into the new courthouse and offices. The two story brick building was located on the hill west of Stark Avenue (which was later renamed Collins Avenue). A sheriff's quarters and jailhouse, located to the west of the new courthouse, was also part of the complex.</p>

<p>After extremely bitter winter temperatures, spring came to the area in late March. The Peoples and Shields stables experienced unusually high demand for buggy rentals for an impromptu Sunday drive, prompted by the mild weather. The work of washing buggies was a leading cottage industry in Mandan during the first part of the year.</p>

<h3>1887</h3>

<p>The city experienced one of the worst floods in its short history. The school was closed for two weeks in March-April due to the flood. Train traffic across the Missouri River was temporarily halted in April after the river ice breakup due to concerns over potential damage on the eastside trestle due to ice chunks.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/300-4-cowboy-picture-cropped.jpg" alt="Four cowboys posed for a photograph">
    <figcaption>(L to R - Sitting) Sylvane Ferris and Gregor Lang; (L-R Standing) Joe Ferris and Bill Merrifield</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>On June 13, Sylvane Ferris and 22 other men rode into town on their way to a round-up in the western part of the territory. They settled in at Curley Sublette's saloon, the popular resort for cowmen and old-timers. Fortunately the group "behaved very nicely while in town" according to newspaper reports. (NOTE: Ferris and his longtime partner Bill Merrifield, both originally from Canada, came to the Badlands in 1883. They lent a horse to a young Theodore Roosevelt for his first hunting trip in Dakota Territory in September 1883.)</p>

<p>They became business partners in the in TR's first ranch; Maltese Cross Ranch six miles south of Medora and built the famous log cabin for him. Ferris and Merrifield were the ranch hands who stayed on the longest, tending the remainder of TR’s cattle after the Elkhorn Ranch closed in 1887. After Merrifield quit in 1892, Ferris continued on alone until Roosevelt finally sold out in 1898. Sylvane and his brother Joe Ferris were two of a handful of residents that remained in Medora after the cattle ranching collapse of 1887.)</p>

<h3>1888</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/222-boneshaker-derated.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Pre-Statehood 1880-1889 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The population of Mandan stood at 2600. Mandan continued to expand as it supported Northern Pacific Railroad operations. In May, eighteen westbound freight trains operated through the city daily. In April a steam engine arrived to power the Mandan Pioneer newspapers presses. Chief Telegrapher Wood purchased a high wheel bicycle, one of the "kicking sort." In October, dry conditions contributed to wild prairie fires throughtout the region. The Mandan Pioneer reported, "Nearly all the land north and northwest of town for a distance of about 20 miles has been burnt over."</p>

<h3>1889</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1880s/154-1885-tr-in-buckskin-derated.jpg" alt="Theodore Roosevelt wearing buckskin in an 1885 portrait">
    <figcaption>1885 TR Culver Portrait</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In October, Theodore Roosevelt passed through the city by train, stopping by to say hello to his longtime friend Dr. Henry Waldo Coe. Roosevelt was returing from a hunting trip out west, where he claimed to have shot two grizzly bears. In November 2, North Dakota joined South Dakota as the 39th and 40th states to join the United States of America. No longer tied to Congressional imposed financial restrictions as a US territory, the City of Mandan opened the wallet and increased municipal debt limit beyond the prior 4% of value to fund the development of an artesion water well to supply the city.</p>
